// Rep Max questions: Physics (AQA 8463), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in provenance/ted-gcse/questions/.
{"id":"gph-001","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A car of mass 800 kg moves at 15 m/s. What is its kinetic energy?","o":["90 000 J","6000 J","180 000 J","12 000 J"],"a":0,"x":"Kinetic energy = ½ × m × v². So ½ × 800 × 15² = ½ × 800 × 225 = 90 000 J. Forgetting to square v, or forgetting the ½, gives the wrong options."}
{"id":"gph-002","s":"physics","t":"4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A motor takes in 250 J of electrical energy. It wastes 60 J as thermal and sound energy. What is its efficiency?","o":["24%","32%","76%","132%"],"a":2,"x":"Useful output = 250 − 60 = 190 J. Efficiency = useful output ÷ total input = 190 ÷ 250 = 0.76, which is 76%. 24% is the wasted fraction."}
{"id":"gph-003","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these energy resources is renewable?","o":["Natural gas","Tidal power","Coal","Uranium for nuclear power"],"a":1,"x":"A renewable resource is replaced as fast as it is used. Tides keep coming. Coal, gas and uranium will run out because they cannot be replaced in a human lifetime."}
{"id":"gph-004","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A resistor has a potential difference of 9.0 V across it and a current of 0.30 A through it. What is its resistance?","o":["2.7 Ω","0.033 Ω","9.3 Ω","30 Ω"],"a":3,"x":"Resistance = potential difference ÷ current (R = V ÷ I). So R = 9.0 ÷ 0.30 = 30 Ω."}
{"id":"gph-005","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A 20 V battery is connected across a 4 Ω resistor and a 6 Ω resistor in series. What is the current in the circuit?","o":["2.0 A","5.0 A","3.3 A","8.3 A"],"a":0,"x":"In series, resistances add: 4 + 6 = 10 Ω. Current = V ÷ R = 20 ÷ 10 = 2.0 A. Using only one resistor gives 5.0 A or 3.3 A."}
{"id":"gph-006","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A 12 V cell is connected across a 6 Ω resistor and a 3 Ω resistor in parallel. What current does the cell supply?","o":["1.3 A","4.0 A","6.0 A","2.0 A"],"a":2,"x":"Each branch has the full 12 V. Branch currents: 12 ÷ 6 = 2.0 A and 12 ÷ 3 = 4.0 A. In parallel the branch currents add to the total, 6.0 A. 1.3 A comes from wrongly adding the resistors."}
{"id":"gph-007","s":"physics","t":"4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"In a UK three-pin plug, which wire is correctly matched with its colour?","o":["Live wire: blue","Neutral wire: blue","Earth wire: brown","Neutral wire: green and yellow"],"a":1,"x":"The live wire is brown, the neutral wire is blue and the earth wire is green and yellow striped."}
{"id":"gph-008","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A kettle runs from the 230 V mains and has a current of 8.0 A. What is its power?","o":["29 W","238 W","14 720 W","1840 W"],"a":3,"x":"Power = potential difference × current (P = V × I). So P = 230 × 8.0 = 1840 W. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 29 W."}
{"id":"gph-009","s":"physics","t":"4.2.5","lv":1,"q":"A plastic rod becomes negatively charged when it is rubbed with a cloth. What has happened?","o":["Electrons moved from the cloth to the rod","Protons moved from the cloth to the rod","Electrons moved from the rod to the cloth","Protons moved from the rod to the cloth"],"a":0,"x":"Only electrons can move in friction charging. The rod gains electrons, so it becomes negative, and the cloth loses them and becomes positive. Protons stay fixed in the nuclei."}
{"id":"gph-010","s":"physics","t":"4.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A block has a mass of 0.60 kg and a volume of 0.00020 m³. What is its density?","o":["300 kg/m³","0.00033 kg/m³","3000 kg/m³","3.0 kg/m³"],"a":2,"x":"Density = mass ÷ volume. So 0.60 ÷ 0.00020 = 3000 kg/m³. Dividing the other way round gives 0.00033."}
{"id":"gph-011","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":2,"q":"How much energy is needed to heat 2.0 kg of water from 20 °C to 70 °C? The specific heat capacity of water is 4200 J/kg °C.","o":["8400 J","420 000 J","588 000 J","210 000 J"],"a":1,"x":"Change in thermal energy = m × c × Δθ = 2.0 × 4200 × (70 − 20) = 420 000 J. The temperature change is 50 °C, not 70 °C."}
{"id":"gph-012","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A sealed container of gas is heated and its volume stays the same. Why does the gas pressure rise?","o":["There are more particles in the container","The particles get bigger and take up more room","The particles lose energy and stick to the walls","The particles move faster and hit the walls harder and more often"],"a":3,"x":"Heating raises the average kinetic energy of the particles. They hit the walls more often and with more force, so the pressure rises. The number of particles has not changed."}
{"id":"gph-013","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3.3","lv":3,"q":"A bicycle pump is pushed in quickly and the air inside gets warmer. Which statement explains this?","o":["Work done on the gas transfers energy to the kinetic energy of its particles","The gas particles gain mass as they are squashed together","Energy is created because the pressure of the gas rises","The particles stop moving and release stored thermal energy"],"a":0,"x":"Pushing the piston does work on the gas. This energy increases the internal energy, mainly the kinetic energy of the particles, so the temperature rises. Energy is not created."}
{"id":"gph-014","s":"physics","t":"4.4.1","lv":1,"q":"How does an atom of carbon-14 differ from an atom of carbon-12?","o":["It has two more protons","It has two more electrons","It has two more neutrons","It has two more protons and two more electrons"],"a":2,"x":"Both are carbon, so both have 6 protons. Isotopes have different numbers of neutrons: carbon-14 has 8 neutrons and carbon-12 has 6."}
{"id":"gph-015","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A source emits radiation that passes through paper but is stopped by thin aluminium. What is the radiation?","o":["Alpha","Beta","Gamma","Neutron"],"a":1,"x":"Alpha is stopped by paper. Beta passes through paper but is stopped by a few mm of aluminium. Gamma and neutron radiation are much more penetrating."}
{"id":"gph-016","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A nucleus of mass number 238 and atomic number 92 emits one alpha particle, then two beta particles. What are the final mass number and atomic number?","o":["234 and 90","230 and 92","238 and 94","234 and 92"],"a":3,"x":"Alpha: mass number −4, atomic number −2 gives 234 and 90. Each beta: mass number unchanged, atomic number +1, so two betas give 234 and 92."}
{"id":"gph-017","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A radioactive source has a half-life of 6 hours. Its count rate is 800 counts per minute at the start. What is it after 18 hours?","o":["100 counts per minute","200 counts per minute","133 counts per minute","400 counts per minute"],"a":0,"x":"18 hours is 3 half-lives. The count rate halves each time: 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 counts per minute."}
{"id":"gph-018","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Which example is irradiation, not contamination?","o":["A worker spills a radioactive liquid on their skin","A person breathes in radioactive dust that stays in the lungs","Food is exposed to gamma rays to kill bacteria","Radioactive atoms get into a river and stay in the water"],"a":2,"x":"Irradiation is exposure to radiation from a source outside the object, and the object does not become radioactive. Contamination means radioactive atoms get onto or into something."}
{"id":"gph-019","s":"physics","t":"4.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a natural source of background radiation?","o":["Nuclear weapons testing","Cosmic rays from space","Medical X-rays","Waste from nuclear power stations"],"a":1,"x":"Cosmic rays, radon gas and rocks are natural sources. Weapons testing, medical X-rays and nuclear waste are human-made sources."}
{"id":"gph-020","s":"physics","t":"4.4.4","lv":1,"q":"Which statement describes nuclear fusion?","o":["A large nucleus splits into two smaller nuclei","A nucleus emits an alpha particle","A neutron in a nucleus changes into a proton","Two light nuclei join to form a heavier nucleus"],"a":3,"x":"Fusion is the joining of light nuclei, such as hydrogen, to make a heavier nucleus and release energy. Splitting a large nucleus is fission."}
{"id":"gph-021","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1","lv":2,"q":"An astronaut has a mass of 70 kg. The gravitational field strength on the Moon is 1.6 N/kg. What is the astronaut's weight on the Moon?","o":["112 N","686 N","70 N","44 N"],"a":0,"x":"Weight = mass × gravitational field strength = 70 × 1.6 = 112 N. 686 N is the weight on Earth (70 × 9.8). Mass stays the same everywhere."}
{"id":"gph-022","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A box is pushed to the right with a force of 40 N. Friction of 15 N acts to the left. What is the resultant force?","o":["55 N to the right","25 N to the left","25 N to the right","15 N to the left"],"a":2,"x":"The forces act in opposite directions, so subtract: 40 − 15 = 25 N. The larger force is to the right, so the resultant is to the right."}
{"id":"gph-023","s":"physics","t":"4.5.2","lv":1,"q":"A person pushes a crate 6.0 m along the floor with a constant force of 80 N. How much work is done?","o":["13 J","480 J","86 J","0.075 J"],"a":1,"x":"Work done = force × distance moved in the direction of the force = 80 × 6.0 = 480 J."}
{"id":"gph-024","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":3,"q":"A spring has a spring constant of 200 N/m. It is compressed by 12 cm, within its limit of proportionality. How much energy is stored?","o":["14 400 J","12 J","2.88 J","1.44 J"],"a":3,"x":"Convert 12 cm to 0.12 m. Elastic potential energy = ½ × k × e² = ½ × 200 × 0.12² = 1.44 J. Leaving out the conversion gives 14 400."}
{"id":"gph-025","s":"physics","t":"4.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A seesaw is balanced. A 300 N force acts 1.5 m to the left of the pivot. What force acts 2.0 m to the right of the pivot?","o":["225 N","400 N","600 N","150 N"],"a":0,"x":"At balance the clockwise moment equals the anticlockwise moment. 300 × 1.5 = 450 N m, so the force on the right is 450 ÷ 2.0 = 225 N."}
{"id":"gph-026","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5","lv":1,"q":"A force of 600 N acts at right angles on an area of 0.030 m². What is the pressure?","o":["18 Pa","2000 Pa","20 000 Pa","0.000 05 Pa"],"a":2,"x":"Pressure = force ÷ area = 600 ÷ 0.030 = 20 000 Pa (N/m²). Multiplying instead of dividing gives 18."}
{"id":"gph-027","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A diver is 15 m below the surface of fresh water of density 1000 kg/m³. What is the pressure due to the water? (g = 9.8 N/kg)","o":["15 000 Pa","147 000 Pa","1470 Pa","14 700 Pa"],"a":1,"x":"Pressure = height × density × g = 15 × 1000 × 9.8 = 147 000 Pa. Leaving out g gives 15 000."}
{"id":"gph-028","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":1,"q":"A runner covers 400 m in 50 s at a constant speed. What is the speed?","o":["0.125 m/s","350 m/s","20 000 m/s","8.0 m/s"],"a":3,"x":"Speed = distance ÷ time = 400 ÷ 50 = 8.0 m/s."}
{"id":"gph-029","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":2,"q":"A car speeds up from 4.0 m/s to 16 m/s in 3.0 s. What is its acceleration?","o":["4.0 m/s²","5.3 m/s²","0.25 m/s²","12 m/s²"],"a":0,"x":"Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time = (16 − 4.0) ÷ 3.0 = 4.0 m/s². 5.3 comes from forgetting the starting speed."}
{"id":"gph-030","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A car moving at 8.0 m/s accelerates uniformly at 2.0 m/s² over a distance of 60 m. What is its final speed?","o":["304 m/s","15 m/s","17 m/s","11 m/s"],"a":2,"x":"Use v² − u² = 2as. v² = 8.0² + 2 × 2.0 × 60 = 64 + 240 = 304, so v = √304 ≈ 17 m/s. Forgetting the square root gives 304; forgetting u² gives 15."}
{"id":"gph-031","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":2,"q":"A 1200 kg car has a resultant force of 3000 N acting on it. What is its acceleration?","o":["0.4 m/s²","2.5 m/s²","3.6 m/s²","4200 m/s²"],"a":1,"x":"Force = mass × acceleration, so acceleration = 3000 ÷ 1200 = 2.5 m/s². Dividing mass by force gives 0.4."}
{"id":"gph-032","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":2,"q":"A book rests on a table. Which force forms a Newton's third law pair with the weight of the book (the Earth's pull on the book)?","o":["The upward contact force of the table on the book","The downward force of the book on the table","The friction between the book and the table","The book's gravitational pull on the Earth"],"a":3,"x":"A third law pair acts on two different objects, is the same type of force and is equal and opposite. Earth pulls the book down, so the book pulls the Earth up."}
{"id":"gph-033","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.3","lv":1,"q":"Which factor increases a driver's thinking distance?","o":["The driver is tired","The road is wet","The tyres are worn","The car has a larger mass"],"a":0,"x":"Thinking distance depends on reaction time, which is longer when a driver is tired or has taken alcohol or drugs. Wet roads, worn tyres and a heavier car increase braking distance."}
{"id":"gph-034","s":"physics","t":"4.5.7","lv":3,"q":"A 1000 kg car moving at 12 m/s hits a stationary 500 kg car. They stick together. What is their speed just after the collision?","o":["12 m/s","6.0 m/s","8.0 m/s","4.0 m/s"],"a":2,"x":"Momentum is conserved. Before: 1000 × 12 = 12 000 kg m/s. After: (1000 + 500) × v = 12 000, so v = 8.0 m/s."}
{"id":"gph-035","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1","lv":2,"q":"A water wave has a period of 0.50 s and a wavelength of 0.80 m. What is its wave speed?","o":["0.40 m/s","1.6 m/s","0.63 m/s","2.0 m/s"],"a":1,"x":"Frequency = 1 ÷ period = 1 ÷ 0.50 = 2.0 Hz. Wave speed = frequency × wavelength = 2.0 × 0.80 = 1.6 m/s. 2.0 is only the frequency."}
{"id":"gph-036","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A ray of light passes from air into a glass block at an angle to the normal. What happens to the ray?","o":["It speeds up and bends towards the normal","It slows down and bends away from the normal","It speeds up and bends away from the normal","It slows down and bends towards the normal"],"a":3,"x":"Light travels more slowly in glass than in air, so it bends towards the normal as it enters the glass. Explaining refraction by the change in speed is Higher tier."}
{"id":"gph-037","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A boat sends an ultrasound pulse down. The echo from the sea bed returns after 0.40 s. Ultrasound travels at 1500 m/s in sea water. How deep is the sea bed?","o":["300 m","600 m","150 m","3750 m"],"a":0,"x":"The pulse travels to the sea bed and back, so total distance = 1500 × 0.40 = 600 m. The depth is half of this, 300 m."}
{"id":"gph-038","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which list shows electromagnetic waves in order of increasing wavelength?","o":["Visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays","Gamma rays, ultraviolet, X-rays, visible light","Gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet, visible light","X-rays, gamma rays, visible light, ultraviolet"],"a":2,"x":"The spectrum runs: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma, from longest to shortest wavelength. Reading it backwards gives increasing wavelength."}
{"id":"gph-039","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.2","lv":3,"q":"What happens when radio waves are absorbed by the aerial of a radio receiver?","o":["A direct current is produced","An alternating current is produced with the same frequency as the waves","An alternating current is produced with a lower frequency","The aerial emits radio waves of a different frequency"],"a":1,"x":"When radio waves are absorbed by a conductor they create an alternating current in it with the same frequency as the radio waves, which the receiver turns into sound."}
{"id":"gph-040","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which pair of electromagnetic waves are ionising and can cause gene mutations and cancer?","o":["Radio waves and microwaves","Infrared and visible light","Microwaves and infrared","X-rays and gamma rays"],"a":3,"x":"X-rays and gamma rays carry enough energy to ionise atoms and damage DNA. Radio waves, microwaves, infrared and visible light do not ionise."}
{"id":"gph-041","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A red T-shirt is looked at in pure green light. What colour does it appear?","o":["Black","Red","Green","White"],"a":0,"x":"A red object reflects red light and absorbs other colours. There is no red light in pure green light, so the T-shirt absorbs the green and reflects nothing: it looks black."}
{"id":"gph-042","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":1,"q":"Which surface is the best emitter of infrared radiation at the same temperature?","o":["Shiny white","Shiny black","Matt black","Matt white"],"a":2,"x":"Matt black surfaces are the best absorbers and the best emitters of infrared radiation. Shiny and light-coloured surfaces are poor emitters."}
{"id":"gph-043","s":"physics","t":"4.7.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about the magnetic field of a bar magnet is correct?","o":["Field lines point from the south pole to the north pole outside the magnet","Field lines point from the north pole to the south pole outside the magnet","Field lines are furthest apart at the poles","Field lines cross each other near the centre"],"a":1,"x":"Field lines leave the north pole and enter the south pole. They are closest together, and so the field is strongest, at the poles. They never cross."}
{"id":"gph-044","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which change makes the magnetic field of a solenoid stronger?","o":["Reducing the current","Taking some turns off the coil","Reversing the direction of the current","Putting an iron core inside the coil"],"a":3,"x":"A solenoid is stronger with more current, more turns, or a soft iron core. Reversing the current only reverses the direction of the field."}
{"id":"gph-045","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A wire of length 0.15 m carries a current of 3.0 A at right angles to a magnetic field of 0.20 T. What force acts on the wire?","o":["0.090 N","4.0 N","0.90 N","0.010 N"],"a":0,"x":"Force = magnetic flux density × current × length (F = BIl) = 0.20 × 3.0 × 0.15 = 0.090 N."}
{"id":"gph-046","s":"physics","t":"4.7.3","lv":3,"q":"An ideal step-down transformer has 230 V across its primary coil and 23 V across its secondary coil. The secondary current is 4.0 A. What is the primary current?","o":["40 A","4.0 A","0.40 A","0.040 A"],"a":2,"x":"For an ideal transformer, Vp × Ip = Vs × Is. So 230 × Ip = 23 × 4.0 = 92, and Ip = 0.40 A. The step-down transformer has a smaller current in the primary."}
{"id":"gph-047","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What was our Solar System formed from?","o":["Material thrown out of the Sun after it formed","A cloud of dust and gas pulled together by gravity","A collision between two galaxies","A black hole that cooled and broke up"],"a":1,"x":"The Sun and planets formed together from a huge cloud of dust and gas (a nebula) that was pulled together by gravity. The planets did not come from the Sun."}
{"id":"gph-048","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Which sequence is correct for a star the same size as our Sun?","o":["Protostar, red giant, main sequence, white dwarf","Protostar, main sequence, red supergiant, neutron star","Main sequence, protostar, red giant, white dwarf","Protostar, main sequence, red giant, white dwarf"],"a":3,"x":"A star like the Sun forms from a cloud as a protostar, spends most of its life on the main sequence, swells into a red giant, then shrinks to a white dwarf."}
{"id":"gph-049","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A satellite is moved into a smaller circular orbit around the Earth. Which statement is correct?","o":["Its orbital speed must increase","Its orbital speed must decrease","Its orbital speed stays the same","It no longer needs a gravitational force"],"a":0,"x":"Gravity is stronger closer to the Earth, so the satellite must move faster to stay in a smaller stable orbit. Its direction keeps changing, so its velocity changes even at constant speed."}
{"id":"gph-050","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":2,"q":"Light from distant galaxies is red-shifted, and the shift is larger for galaxies that are further away. What does this suggest?","o":["Distant galaxies are moving towards us faster","All the galaxies are the same distance away","Distant galaxies are moving away faster, so the Universe is expanding","The Universe is getting smaller"],"a":2,"x":"Red-shift means the galaxies are moving away. A larger red-shift for more distant galaxies shows they are receding faster, which is evidence that the Universe is expanding."}
